* Their other calls after lane D2's tables (NOTES.md #C4, #P7)

Phase 4 (the resolve_lenient investigation, docs/design/12-roadmap.md). The
tables lane D2 added (NOTES.md #P12) describe our next call after an
opponent's pass. When the opponent bids or doubles at that point instead, the
trie has no edge for that call, and `resolve_lenient` reads it "as if they
had passed": the table is applied to an auction it was not written for, whose
calls are partly illegal and partly wrong in meaning. This is the hole NOTES.md
#C4 closes for the uncontested tables and #P7 for the competitive ones; on the
corpus it took the lenient count from 1 to 61 calls.

Each history line below gives such a position an `(any)` sibling of the pass
with no rows (`OppClass::AnyCall`, the extension token #C4 uses). The trie
still follows the exact pass to the table; any other call reaches the empty
position, which is off-system, so the next call is bid by natural inference.
Positions that a system stop already covers (the stop's own `(any)` step) are
not listed. The lines live in a file of their own so that the tables they
guard stay as lane D2 wrote them; `crates/bridge-system/tests/sayc.rs` checks
that none of D2's tables is left without one.

